<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Syrian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Syria is a historical region of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, lying between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Its cities, trade routes and fertile lands made it one of the most contested regions in history, fought over by the Hittite, Egyptian, Assyrian, Seleucid, Ptolemaic, Roman and Byzantine powers. In the Islamic period it was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and later a core territory of the Ayyubid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/syria.jpg" alt="Syria" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Modification by AnonMoos of PD image File:Flag of Syria (1930–1958, 1961–1963).svg (previous non-vector versions were by Coup de … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Syria_(2025-).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The modern state of Syria covers 185,180 km2 in the Eastern Mediterranean and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. Its land runs from the Mediterranean coastal plains to the mountains of the west, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hermon">Mount Hermon</a>, at 2,814 metres, is the highest point, and much of the centre and east is arid plateau and desert. The valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, Orontes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> rivers support agriculture and settlement. Commercial petroleum was first discovered in the northeast in 1956.</p><p>The name Syria is borrowed from Ancient Greek, which took it from Luwian sura/i-, attested in the 8th century BCE, and mainstream scholarship relates it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> applied it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> without distinguishing the Assyrians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> from the Arameans of the Levant. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> the larger Syria was divided into provinces such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>, renamed Palaestina in 135 CE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenice">Phoenice</a>, established in 194.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Natufian culture became sedentary around the 11th millennium BCE, and the site of Tell Qaramel has round stone towers dated to 10650 BCE. The earliest recorded indigenous civilisation was the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a>, near present-day Idlib, which appears to have been founded around 3500 BCE and grew rich through trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> and contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>One of its early texts, the Treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abarsal">Abarsal</a> of about 2300 BCE, is a trading agreement. Ebla was weakened by a long war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, and in the first half of the 23rd century BCE the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> ended its domination of Syria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The modern state emerged from a French Mandate. It gained de jure independence in 1945 as a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, and French troops withdrew in April 1946. Coups and coup attempts followed between 1949 and 1971, and a union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> lasted from 1958 to 1961. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baath-party">Ba'ath Party</a> took power in 1963. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez-al-assad">Hafez al-Assad</a> came to power in 1970 and was succeeded on his death in 2000 by his son Bashar. A civil war began in 2011, and the Assad government fell in December 2024.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Syria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Perhaps 100,000 Arabs then lived in Ifriqiya, most of them immigrants from <b>Syria</b> and Iraq, but the Berbers remained the great majority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He then campaigned into Elam to the east and up the Euphrates into <b>Syria</b>, reaching the Cedar Forest and the Silver Mountains towards the Mediterranean, while later legend carried his armies into Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Antigonus I Monophthalmus, ‘the One-Eyed’, one of Alexander the Great's senior officers, had governed Phrygia since 333 BCE, and in the wars of the Successors after 323 BCE he built up the largest of the competing realms, stretching from western Asia Minor to <b>Syria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Nicephorus II Phocas retook Cilicia from the Arabs in 965 the Byzantines settled Christians from <b>Syria</b> and Armenia there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Second World War about 150,000 Armenians from countries such as Cyprus, France, Greece, Iraq, Lebanon and <b>Syria</b> came between 1946 and 1948.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He took Damascus that autumn and absorbed the Zengid principalities of <b>Syria</b> and northern Mesopotamia one after another, by military pressure and by diplomacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
